<%--
Add_Api_Key_Modal_Form - Modal form for creating API keys

Collects the key name and the key's ACCESS: unrestricted (the key carries every permission
its holder has) or scoped (the key is narrowed to a rule set, and can never reach anything
outside it).

The form declares its endpoint on the <Rsx_Form> tag - the single place the endpoint is
named - and the hosting Modal.form() drives that form's submit(). VALIDATION IS THE
SERVER'S, including every scope: create_key hands the union to Api_Scopes and answers a
malformed scope under the `scopes` field, in the framework validator's own words. The panel
below the fields is a PREVIEW, not a check - it shows what the scopes reach, and it asks the
server, which is the same implementation the dispatcher matches with.

Keys are immutable after mint: there is no edit form anywhere, because narrowing a
credential already in service would change its meaning under the integration holding it.
Revoke and mint a replacement instead.
--%>
<Define:Add_Api_Key_Modal_Form>

    <Rsx_Form $sid="form" $controller="Frontend_Settings_Api_Keys_Controller" $method="create_key">

        <Form_Errors />

        <Form_Field $label="Key Name" $required=true
            $help="Give your key a descriptive name so you can identify it later.">
            <Text_Input $name="name" $max_length=255 $placeholder="e.g., Production API, CI/CD Integration" $autofocus=true />
        </Form_Field>

        <Form_Field $label="Access" $required=true>
            <Select_With_Description_Input $sid="access_mode" $name="access_mode" $options=this.data.access_options />
        </Form_Field>

        <%-- Present on every render and merely hidden while the key is unrestricted: the
             inputs inside it still serialize, and the server ignores them for an
             unrestricted key. A branch would destroy the operator's half-written scopes
             every time they looked at the other mode. --%>
        <div $sid="scoped_section" class="Add_Api_Key_Modal_Form__scoped">

            <Form_Field $label="Presets">
                <Api_Scope_Presets_Input $sid="presets" $name="presets" $options=this.data.presets />
            </Form_Field>

            <Form_Field $label="Custom scopes"
                $help="One path pattern per line. Each one is a GRANT - there is no way to write a denial, because a key with any scope at all can reach nothing else.">
                <Text_Input $sid="custom_scopes" $name="scopes" $type="textarea" $rows=4 $max_length=-1
                    $placeholder="/api/v1/tasks/*" />
            </Form_Field>

            <%-- The whole grammar, in the one place somebody is typing it. Written out rather
                 than linked: an operator narrowing a credential is not going to leave the
                 dialog to read a man page, and a scope they guess wrong at is a key that
                 quietly reaches more or less than they meant. --%>
            <div class="Add_Api_Key_Modal_Form__grammar">
                <div class="Add_Api_Key_Modal_Form__grammar_title">How a scope is written</div>
                <ul>
                    <li>A scope is a path, starting <code>/api/</code> then a version:
                        <code>/api/v1/clients/42/view</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>?</code> stands for exactly one segment of any kind:
                        <code>/api/v1/clients/?/view</code>. It is allowed as the version too
                        (<code>/api/?/clients</code>), for a key that should follow the API
                        into its next version.</li>
                    <li><code>#</code> stands for exactly one segment of digits:
                        <code>/api/v1/clients/#/view</code> reaches client 42 and not client
                        "settings".</li>
                    <li><code>*</code> stands for the rest of the path and may only be the
                        LAST segment. It covers the prefix itself as well as everything below
                        it: <code>/api/v1/clients/*</code> reaches
                        <code>/api/v1/clients</code> and
                        <code>/api/v1/clients/42/attachments</code> alike.</li>
                    <li>Each wildcard is a WHOLE segment. <code>/api/v1/cli*</code>,
                        <code>/api/v1/?_id</code> and <code>/api/v1/x#</code> are rejected -
                        they would look like patterns and match nothing.</li>
                    <li>A trailing slash means nothing on either side, and a query string is
                        never part of the match: <code>/api/v1/clients</code>,
                        <code>/api/v1/clients/</code> and
                        <code>/api/v1/clients?page=2</code> are one and the same path.</li>
                    <li>Everything else is matched literally, letter for letter.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="Add_Api_Key_Modal_Form__grammar_title">How they are applied</div>
                <ul>
                    <li>Every scope GRANTS. The moment a key has one, everything it does not
                        name is refused - so there is nothing to write a denial for.</li>
                    <li>A scope never widens the key beyond what your account may do. It can
                        only take away.</li>
                    <li>A scope that does not follow the rules above is refused here. One that
                        somehow reaches the key anyway is ignored for matching and STILL
                        counts, so such a key can call nothing at all.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <Api_Scope_Preview $sid="preview" $scopes="" />

        </div>

    </Rsx_Form>

</Define:Add_Api_Key_Modal_Form>
